## Limitations

This is a simple prototype rather than a full production system. Some limitations include:

- no database storage
- no real authentication
- no persistent data between runs
- no user-friendly interface
- no advanced reporting or admin features

Security for borrowed items works in layers: who can borrow, who can release an item, what happens if it is damaged, and what record is kept. Here is how it works in the Flask and MySQL version, and then how much of it carries over to the simple Python file.

Before the item is lent
Only real residents get in. Only the 20 real rooms exist in the database, and each room has a one-time invite code that the admin hands out. To register you need the room number and the code, and each room can register only once. Outsiders can’t create accounts.
Borrowers are screened. Every request is checked against the eligibility rules before it is created. The borrower must be active, have fewer than 3 strikes, have no overdue item, have fewer than 2 active loans, and the item must be available and requested within the owner’s maximum days. If any rule fails, the request is refused.
The owner decides. Nothing is lent automatically. The owner must approve each request.
Double booking is blocked. When the owner approves, the app checks that no other loan for the same item is already approved or out.

## Summary

The Victor Hostel Borrow and Lend System is a practical Python project that simulates a hostel item-sharing marketplace. It teaches important programming principles while solving a genuine need in a student accommodation environment.

The app helps students borrow and lend useful items safely, with rules and tracking to prevent misuse.
